WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Part 3 You will hear two business students discussing

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:12.400
automation and the future of work. First you have some time to look at questions 21-24.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21-24.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.919
That seminar yesterday on automation and the future of work was really good, wasn't it?

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:52.439
Looking at the first industrial revolution in Britain in the 19th century and seeing

00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:56.320
how people reacted to massive change was a real eye-opener.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.599
Yes, it was interesting to hear how people felt about automation then and what challenges

00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:07.959
they faced. I didn't know that first started with workers in the textile industry.

00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:13.580
With those protesting workers called the Luddites destroying their knitting machines because

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.279
they were so worried about losing their jobs.

00:01:16.279 --> 00:01:22.440
Yes, and ultimately they didn't achieve anything. And anyway, industrialisation created more

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.959
jobs than it destroyed.

00:01:23.959 --> 00:01:29.680
Yes, that's true. But it probably didn't seem a positive thing at the time. I can see

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.720
why the Luddites felt so threatened.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:37.440
I know. I'm sure I would have felt the same. The discussion about the future of work was

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.320
really optimistic for a change. I like the idea that work won't involve doing boring,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.519
repetitive tasks as robots will do all that. Normally you only hear negative stuff about

00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:50.519
the future.

00:01:50.559 --> 00:01:55.639
You're a bit too optimistic, don't you think? For example, I can't see how people are about

00:01:55.639 --> 00:02:00.519
to have more leisure time when all the evidence shows people are spending longer than ever

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:01.519
at work.

00:02:01.519 --> 00:02:07.519
No, that's true. And what about lower unemployment? I'm not so sure about that.

00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:12.720
Perhaps in the long term, but not in the foreseeable future.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.119
And I expect most people will be expected to work until they're much older, as everyone's

00:02:17.119 --> 00:02:18.759
living much longer.

00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:23.440
That's already happening.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.479
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25-30.

00:02:48.759 --> 00:03:11.759
I enjoyed all that stuff on how technology has changed some jobs, and how they're likely

00:03:11.759 --> 00:03:13.759
to change in the near future.

00:03:14.759 --> 00:03:20.160
Incredible. Like accountants. You might think all the technological innovations would have

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.000
put them out of a job, but in fact there are more of them than ever. They're still really

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.759
in demand and have become far more efficient.

00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:34.759
Right. That was amazing. 20 times more accountants in this country compared to the 19th century.

00:03:34.759 --> 00:03:39.139
I know. I'd never have thought that demand for hairdressing would have gone up so much

00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:46.020
in the last hundred years. One hairdresser for every 287 people now, compared to one

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.139
for over 1,500.

00:03:48.139 --> 00:03:53.139
Yeah, because people's earning power has gone up so they can afford to spend more on personal

00:03:53.139 --> 00:03:54.820
services like that.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.139
But technology hasn't changed the actual job that much.

00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:04.339
No. They've got hairdryers, etc. But it's one job where you don't depend on a computer.

00:04:04.339 --> 00:04:09.339
The kind of work that administrative staff do has changed enormously, thanks to technology.

00:04:09.339 --> 00:04:14.339
Even 20 years ago there were secretaries doing dictation and typing.

00:04:14.339 --> 00:04:19.339
Yes. Really boring compared to these days, when they're given much more responsibility

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:21.339
and higher status.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:26.339
A lot of graduates go in for this kind of work now. I'd expected there to be a much

00:04:26.339 --> 00:04:32.339
bigger change in the number of agricultural workers in the 19th century, but the 1871

00:04:32.339 --> 00:04:37.339
census showed that roughly 25% of the population worked on the land.

00:04:37.339 --> 00:04:43.339
Yeah, I'd have assumed it would be more than 50%. Now it's less than 0.2%.

00:04:43.339 --> 00:04:45.339
What about care workers?

00:04:45.339 --> 00:04:50.339
They barely existed in the 19th century, as people's lifespan was so much shorter. But

00:04:50.339 --> 00:04:54.339
now, of course, this sector will see huge growth.

00:04:54.339 --> 00:04:58.339
Yeah, and it's hard enough to meet current demand.

00:04:58.339 --> 00:05:03.339
The future looks quite bleak for bank clerks. They've been in decline since ATMs were

00:05:03.339 --> 00:05:04.339
introduced in the 80s.

00:05:04.339 --> 00:05:09.339
And technology will certainly make most of the jobs they do now redundant, I think.

00:05:09.339 --> 00:05:15.339
I agree. Although the situation may change, it's very hard to predict what will happen.

00:05:18.339 --> 00:05:25.339
That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.

00:05:28.339 --> 00:05:30.339
Thank you.

